WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.590
MAX SALTONSTALL: On this
episode of "Stack Chat,"

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.750
Spotify Infrastructure
Engineer Matt Brown

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.120
talks about how his team
approached the move to Google

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.440
Cloud and Kubernetes.

00:00:10.473 --> 00:00:11.640
Thanks for joining me, Matt.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.340
So I want you to tell
me, first, just how

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.058
is Spotify using GCP behind
the scenes a little bit?

00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:18.600
MATT BROWN: So at
Spotify we use what

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.380
feels like every part of
GCP in some area of Spotify.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.680
And we've moved over the
past couple of years really

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:27.720
everything we do to GCP.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.300
So we use products
on the data side--

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.530
Bigtable, Cloud SQL, BigQuery
pops up for many things.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.490
The area that I
work in, which is

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.640
an infrastructure for
back end services,

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.690
historically we use a lot
of Google computation,

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:43.560
But that's moving mostly over
to Kubernetes engine now.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.935
MAX SALTONSTALL:
And then you had

00:00:44.935 --> 00:00:47.670
to choose between Kubernetes
and managed Kubernetes on GCP.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.520
So how did that choice
play out for you?

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.270
MATT BROWN: So really,
as we were starting

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:54.540
to think and plan for when do
we want to move to Kubernetes,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.150
we were already moving away
from having our own data

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.030
centers to running pretty much
everything in Google Cloud.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.570
So the idea of setting up
our own Kubernetes clusters,

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.510
learning how to manage
the networking, all

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.130
of those things,
just never really

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.230
sounded very appealing to us.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.570
The big promise for
Google Cloud for us

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.830
is all of the managed
services we can use.

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:16.290
And then our
engineers can move up

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.880
a layer in what we're
worrying about and not

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.713
worrying about the low
layer things so much.

00:01:19.713 --> 00:01:22.230
So a managed service like GKE
was really perfect for us.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.230
MAX SALTONSTALL: So what
other kinds of benefits

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:26.880
did your engineering teams get
then from making that shift?

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.360
MATT BROWN: So a huge one is
that in our current platform

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.070
in HELIOS, it's not very
easy to do auto scaling.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.220
So teams tend to have to do
capacity planning every once

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:38.670
in a while to figure out how
many virtual machines would

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:40.960
they need for peak traffic,
something like that.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.710
And then as a result, most
teams are over-provisioned

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.165
for a large amount of the time.

00:01:45.165 --> 00:01:47.790
So auto scaling helps a lot with
that because not only can they

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.783
respond to shifts in
traffic a lot more faster,

00:01:50.783 --> 00:01:52.950
but they also just don't
have to be over-provisioned

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.117
and using so many resources.

00:01:54.117 --> 00:01:56.200
MAX SALTONSTALL: As you're
making this transition,

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.800
how does this system handle
failure or degradation

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.720
of any of the
infrastructure or pieces?

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.420
MATT BROWN: So we use
regional clusters in GKE

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.960
so we can be deployed
across many zones.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.800
Spotify runs across three
different regions in GCP.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.100
So we're just relying on
those primitives, in a way,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.260
for that type of reliability.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.180
And then we also, as
an infrastructure team,

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.890
do a lot of practicing
around let's think

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.290
of a possible disaster--

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.480
somebody deleting a
cluster in the Cloud

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.100
console on accident, let's say--

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.930
let's practice
those things to make

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.360
sure we have good playbooks,
we know how to recover,

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.800
we can catch those with alerts.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.270
Because we figure
eventually it's

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:34.812
going to happen in
production anyway,

00:02:34.812 --> 00:02:36.650
so why not give us a
chance to practice it?

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:37.350
MAX SALTONSTALL: Are
there other things

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:40.260
that you could have done,
or encouraged others to do,

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.380
that would have
sped up this change?

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.150
MATT BROWN: I
would say one thing

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.103
that we found out
in doing this, going

00:02:47.103 --> 00:02:49.770
through this whole process, that
we were a little bit pleasantly

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:54.060
surprised at is we didn't have
to do as much evangelizing

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:57.390
or motivation or having a
carrot stick to tell people

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:58.980
you need to move to Kubernetes.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.790
Most engineering teams
were really excited.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:03.463
And one thing that we found
worked the best was we

00:03:03.463 --> 00:03:05.880
had a group of engineers who
weren't on the infrastructure

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:06.440
team--

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.030
they were just early adopters--
and they did a lot of work

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:12.547
in talking to other
engineers, writing blog posts,

00:03:12.547 --> 00:03:14.130
giving talks at an
internal conference

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.150
we have to motivate
other people.

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:18.650
So one thing that I think we
could've done a little bit more

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:21.515
of was just trying to build
more of those champions

00:03:21.515 --> 00:03:22.140
or evangelists.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:23.650
It was a really
pleasant surprise,

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:24.950
but we could have
always had more.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:25.560
MAX SALTONSTALL: Awesome.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.340
Well, it sounds like
you're on the way,

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.728
and I look forward to hearing
more about it as you get

00:03:29.728 --> 00:03:31.020
closer and closer to your goal.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:32.603
Thanks very much for
joining me today.

00:03:32.603 --> 00:03:33.990
MATT BROWN: Thanks
for having me.

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:35.130
MAX SALTONSTALL: If you
enjoyed this episodes,

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.297
check out the "Stack Chat"
playlist for more videos.

00:03:37.297 --> 00:03:40.460
